INT. KABIR'S BEDROOM - DAY

A wooden clock sits on a desk. Its gears spin slowly. It is missing its central drive gear.

KABIR stands over it. He is a ten-year-old South Asian boy. He wears a green bomber jacket over a white tee and cargo shorts.

His canvas satchel rests on the chair. It is covered in colorful patches.

Kabir pulls a brass folding ruler from his pocket.

Click. Clack. He folds it into a triangle. Then a square.

He measures the empty space in the clock.

KABIR
Just one piece left. The Golden Gear.

He looks at a school newsletter on his desk. It has a picture of a shiny brass gear.

The headline reads: FOUNDER'S GOLDEN GEAR ON DISPLAY IN BASEMENT ARCHIVE.

Kabir stares at the words. He tries to connect the letters to their sounds.

The letters are flat. The spelling patterns do not match the sounds in his head. The word "display" feels like a locked box.

He sighs. He folds his ruler shut.

INT. SCHOOL HALLWAY - DAY

Kabir walks down the busy corridor. He clutches his satchel.

LEO jogs up beside him. Leo holds a thick history book.

LEO
Did you see the announcement? The Golden Gear is up for grabs today. Whoever solves the basement archive puzzle gets to keep it.

KABIR
I need that gear. For my clock.

LEO
No way you will get it first. The puzzle is made of written riddles. I can read a page in ten seconds.

Leo taps his book.

LEO
By the time you finish the first sentence, I will have the gear in my backpack.

Leo laughs and runs ahead.

Kabir stops. He touches the strap of his satchel. He assumes he will be last.

INT. LEARNING SUPPORT CLASSROOM - DAY

Bright green plants sit on the windowsill.

MS. STERLING sits at a low table with Kabir.

A shallow wooden tray filled with fine blue sand sits between them.

Ms. Sterling taps a green card with the word "SAID" written on it.

MS. STERLING
Let us look at this word, Kabir. It is a tricky one. It does not play by the rules.

KABIR
It sounds like /s/ /e/ /d/. But it has an 'a' and an 'i' in the middle.

MS. STERLING
Exactly. The spelling pattern does not match the speech sounds we expect. Let us trace it in the sand. Say the letter names as you trace.

Kabir extends his pointer finger. He presses it into the blue sand.

KABIR
S. A. I. D.

He sweeps his hand under the word.

KABIR
Said.

MS. STERLING
Beautiful. How does your brain feel when we do this?

KABIR
Reading feels like dragging a heavy wooden sled through dry mud. Every word is a weight. But tracing helps me feel the shapes of the letters.

MS. STERLING
Your brain works hard to build those bridges, Kabir. Having dyslexia means your brain processes language differently. But it also means you see the world in three dimensions.

KABIR
I just want to find the gear. Leo says I will be too slow.

MS. STERLING
Speed is not the only way to solve a puzzle. Trust your eyes. Trust your hands.

INT. SCHOOL LIBRARY - AFTERNOON

Kabir stands before a glass display case.

Inside is an ancient, heavy brass map. It is thick like a book.

The map has sliding metal plates. Letters are engraved on the plates.

Leo stands nearby, staring through the glass. He scribbles on a notepad.

LEO
It makes no sense. The riddle on the side says: Slide the stones to find the path. But the words on the sliding plates are scrambled. O-N-E does not fit the slots.

Kabir steps closer. He looks at the brass map.

He does not see flat letters. He sees depth. He sees how the metal plates overlap.

He pulls out his folding ruler.

Click. Clack. He opens it. He aligns it with the glass.

KABIR
They are not just letters. They are interlocking puzzle pieces.

LEO
They are words, Kabir. You have to read them to slide them. And you cannot even read the sign on the wall.

Leo points to a sign: ARCHIVE CLOSED TO PUBLIC.

Kabir feels a pinch in his chest. He remembers his fear. Slow at reading means slow at everything.

But he looks back at the brass map. The shapes fit together in his mind.

The librarian, MR. BEATTY, walks by.

MR. BEATTY
The display is closing, boys. Head home.

Leo runs off toward the main exit.

Kabir lingers. He watches Mr. Beatty lock the case and set the key on the desk.

Kabir hides behind a bookshelf.

INT. SCHOOL LIBRARY - LATER

The library is empty and dim.

Kabir slips out from behind the books. He walks to the desk.

He grabs the key. He unlocks the display case.

He lifts the heavy brass map. It is cold and heavy.

He slides his fingers over the brass plates. He does not try to sound out the words yet. He feels the grooves.

He slides a plate. It clicks.

A hidden compartment on the bottom of the map pops open.

A small brass key sits inside.

KABIR
The key to the basement tunnels.

A shadow falls over him.

Leo stands at the library door.

LEO
I knew you would stay. And you found the key.

KABIR
I worked out how the pieces move.

LEO
Great. Now let us see who gets to the gear first.

Leo snatches the key from Kabir's hand and runs down the hallway.

Kabir grabs his satchel and chases after him.

INT. BASEMENT CORRIDOR - CONTINUOUS

Dim yellow lights flicker along the concrete ceiling.

Leo runs toward a heavy wooden door. He shoves the brass key into the lock.

The key turns. The door creaks open.

Kabir catches up, catching his breath.

KABIR
Wait! The map shows traps.

LEO
I can read the warning signs fine, Kabir.

Leo steps inside.

INT. THE TUNNEL OF GEARS - CONTINUOUS

The room is vast. Huge rusty gears line the walls.

In the center, a stone pathway leads to a pedestal.

On the pedestal sits the Golden Gear. It shines under a single spotlight.

Leo runs onto the stone pathway.

Clack!

A stone sinks under his foot.

The gears on the walls begin to grind.

The stone pathway starts to slide apart. A gap opens over a dark pit.

Leo stops. He wobbles on the edge of a shifting stone.

LEO
Help! The floor is moving!

Kabir looks at the wall.

A large brass panel is set into the stone.

It has six giant sliding metal blocks. Each block has a letter on it.

A plaque above it reads: Spell the name of the creators to lock the floor.

Leo yells over the grinding gears.

LEO
It is a word! What is the word? Look at the sign on the pillar!

Kabir looks at the stone pillar.

An old inscription is carved there.

It is an irregular word: PEOPLE.

Leo tries to read it.

LEO
P-E-E-P-U-L? No! P-E-O-P-L-E? How do you spell that? It does not sound right!

Leo tries to slide the blocks. He slides P, then E, then E.

The gears grind louder. The floor shifts further.

LEO
The letters do not match the sounds! I do not know what goes next!

Kabir runs to the panel.

He looks at the blocks. He remembers the sand tray. He remembers the spelling patterns he practiced with Ms. Sterling.

He remembers how people looks in his mind—not as flat lines, but as a sequence of shapes.

KABIR
It is not spelled how it sounds, Leo.

LEO
Then how do we slide them? Hurry!

Kabir grabs the first block. He slides P into place.

He visualizes the word.

He slides E.

He slides O.

LEO
An O? That makes no sense!

KABIR
It does to me.

Kabir slides P.

He slides L.

He slides E.

The letters spell: P-E-O-P-L-E.

Click.

The grinding stops.

The shifting stones slide back together. The pathway becomes solid.

Leo sighs and sinks to his knees on the stone floor.

LEO
How did you do that? You did not even sound it out.

KABIR
I know the shapes of the tricky words. I practice them because they are hard for me.

Kabir walks down the stable pathway.

He reaches the pedestal.

He lifts the Golden Gear. It is perfectly weighted. It fits his hand.

He smiles.

INT. KABIR'S BEDROOM - NEXT DAY

The sun shines through the window.

Kabir sits at his desk.

He places the Golden Gear into the center of his wooden clock.

He turns a small wooden key on the back.

The clock ticks. The hands move smoothly.

Click. Clack. Kabir opens his folding ruler. He smiles at the ticking sound.

His school history book lies open next to the clock.

He looks at the first sentence.

He struggles to connect the sounds to the letters.

He takes a deep breath. He does not look discouraged.

He pulls out a green index card.

He writes a word on it. He traces the letters with his finger.

KABIR
One step at a time.

The clock ticks on.